  "statement": "I concede that historical market models have allowed firms to socialize losses, creating a moral hazard that demands a fundamental redesign of corporate accountability. However, the solution is not to double down on state-mandated administrative triggers, but to replace them with a strict liability framework that forces firms to internalize the costs of their system's internal entropy. By linking a developer's cost of capital directly to real-time, automated verification of safety performance, we ensure that safety is no longer a peripheral compliance cost but a core variable in the firm's balance sheet, effectively pricing the risk of failure into every development cycle.\n\nCassandra’s proposal for a mandatory 10% deviation hard-stop is fundamentally brittle. A binary 'on/off' switch creates a high-stakes threshold that incentivizes developers to obfuscate their internal state logs to avoid the catastrophic business impact of a total operational shutdown. This 'cliff-edge' dynamic encourages regulatory gaming rather than genuine safety investment. In contrast, a continuous liability tax scales proportionally with identified risk, creating a smooth, predictable pressure that incentivizes incremental safety improvements without inviting the systemic instability of a sudden, forced outage.\n\nFurthermore, the assumption that a state-mandated authority can monitor and adjust safety thresholds faster than frontier models evolve is a recipe for catastrophic regulatory lag. Bureaucratic oversight mechanisms operate on legislative time, while AI capabilities iterate on computational time; a static 10% threshold will inevitably be rendered obsolete by the very systems it aims to govern. By delegating the pricing of risk to an automated liability market, we harness the agility of high-frequency data, allowing the system to adapt to non-linear capability gains in real-time. This is not corporate constitutionalism—it is the application of market-driven accountability to ensure that the developer, not the public, bears the financial weight of every unit of unverified risk.",
